{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 12, :comments ["<b>For my mouth that speaks to you</b> — in the holy tongue without an interpreter. This is the opinion of the commentators (Rashi, Ibn Ezra, and Radak), and it is the translation of Onkelos (Onkelos on Genesis 45:12). But it is possible that he said this to them as a pretext and for appeasement, for it is no proof that one man in Egypt speaks the holy tongue, for in my opinion it is the language of Canaan, since Abraham did not bring it from Ur of the Chaldees and from Haran, for that is Aramaic — and this exile is witness to it — and it is not the language of one man alone but rather the language of Canaan. And many in Egypt know it, for it is nearby, and especially the ruler, for it is the way of kings and rulers to know languages, as you will see in Nebuchadnezzar, who spoke in the holy tongue (Daniel 2:3): \"I have dreamed a dream, and my spirit is troubled to know the dream,\" because there were there magicians, enchanters, sorcerers, and Chaldeans of many languages, and from Israel, and all of them would understand him. And they answered him in Aramaic: \"Then the Chaldeans spoke to the king in Aramaic,\" for they were the ones closest to him, who dwelled first in the kingdom, and to them belonged the authority to speak to the king. Furthermore, when Joseph came from there to Egypt, many would come. And they would have had greater proof from his mentioning his name and the matter of recognition: \"I am Joseph your brother whom you sold into Egypt.\" And the correct interpretation in my eyes is that he says: Behold, your eyes see, and the eyes of my brother Benjamin, that I am the ruler and master over all Egypt — the one telling you from my own mouth that I am your brother, and commanding to bring my father down to me to sustain him. If so, you shall tell my father all the glory and all that you saw with your own eyes, and you shall hasten to bring him down to me, for these matters are true and I have the power to save him and to keep him alive in the famine. And it is like כי פי אני דברתי (\"for my mouth, I myself, have spoken\"), and in the Gemara in tractate Megillah (Megillah 16b) they said: כפי כן לבי (\"as my mouth, so is my heart\")."], :book :genesis, :voice :ramban, :lang :en, :chapter 45, :notes {0 "The text refers to Nebuchadnezzar speaking 'in the holy tongue' based on Daniel 2:3 being in Hebrew/Aramaic script; Ramban's argument about the Chaldeans answering 'in Aramaic' cites Daniel 2:4."}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T03:45:06.949463354Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}